{"description":"<p>One of the great American novels, <i>The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn</i> tells the story of Huck Finn and his travels with Jim, an escaped slave. Roundly criticised by contemporary reviewers for its colorful and literal language and even banned by several libraries, it sealed its historical importance in part by being one of the first novels to be written entirely in American vernacular.</p><p>While <i>Huck Finn</i> is, on its face, an adventure tale for younger readers, it’s also a cutting satire and a nuanced examination of racism and morality. Hemingway called it “the best book we’ve had.”</p>","chapters":[{"title":"Notice","words":46},{"title":"Explanatory","words":94},{"title":"Chapter I","words":1433},{"title":"Chapter II","words":2395},{"title":"Chapter III","words":1710},{"title":"Chapter IV","words":1390},{"title":"Chapter V","words":1662},{"title":"Chapter VI","words":3004},{"title":"Chapter VII","words":2718},{"title":"Chapter VIII","words":4566},{"title":"Chapter IX","words":1626},{"title":"Chapter X","words":1452},{"title":"Chapter XI","words":2905},{"title":"Chapter XII","words":2888},{"title":"Chapter XIII","words":2049},{"title":"Chapter XIV","words":1536},{"title":"Chapter XV","words":2455},{"title":"Chapter XVI","words":3449},{"title":"Chapter XVII","words":3371},{"title":"Chapter XVIII","words":4821},{"title":"Chapter XIX","words":3370},{"title":"Chapter XX","words":3594},{"title":"Chapter XXI","words":3607},{"title":"Chapter XXII","words":2073},{"title":"Chapter XXIII","words":2294},{"title":"Chapter XXIV","words":2351},{"title":"Chapter XXV","words":2866},{"title":"Chapter XXVI","words":2929},{"title":"Chapter XXVII","words":2654},{"title":"Chapter XXVIII","words":3636},{"title":"Chapter XXIX","words":3750},{"title":"Chapter XXX","words":1218},{"title":"Chapter XXXI","words":3732},{"title":"Chapter XXXII","words":2319},{"title":"Chapter XXXIII","words":2672},{"title":"Chapter XXXIV","words":2197},{"title":"Chapter XXXV","wo